public class Application {
    public static fflib_Application.UnitOfWorkFactory UnitOfWork { get; private set; }
    public static fflib_Application.ServiceFactory Service { get; private set; }
    public static fflib_Application.SelectorFactory Selector { get; private set; }
    public static fflib_Application.DomainFactory Domain { get; private set; }

    static {
        Selector = new ApplicationSelectorFactory();
        Domain = new ApplicationDomainFactory();
        Service = new ApplicationServiceFactory();
        UnitOfWork = new ApplicationUnitOfWorkFactory();
    }

    private class ApplicationUnitOfWorkFactory extends fflib_SObjectUnitOfWorkFactory {
        protected override void registerSObjectTypes() {
            registerSObjectType(Account.SObjectType);
            registerSObjectType(Contact.SObjectType);
            registerSObjectType(Opportunity.SObjectType);
            registerSObjectType(OpportunityContactRole.SObjectType);
        }
    }

    private class ApplicationServiceFactory extends fflib_Application.ServiceFactory {
        public ApplicationServiceFactory() {
            registerService(IAccountsService.class, new AccountsServiceImpl());
        }
    }

    private class ApplicationSelectorFactory extends fflib_Application.SelectorFactory {
        public ApplicationSelectorFactory() {
            registerSelector(Account.SObjectType, new AccountsSelector());
            registerSelector(Contact.SObjectType, new ContactsSelector());
        }
    }

    private class ApplicationDomainFactory extends fflib_Application.DomainFactory {
        public ApplicationDomainFactory() {
            registerDomain(Accounts.class, new Accounts.Constructor());
        }
    }
}
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Concern | How it's addressed |
|---------|-------------------|
| **Relationships in UOW** | `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` automatically builds a dependency graph from the schema (Contact→Account, Opportunity→Account, OCR→Opportunity/Contact) and orders DML: Accounts first, then Contacts & Opportunities, then OpportunityContactRoles. |
| **Domain queries via Selector** | `Selector` is initialized before `Domain` in the static block, so when `ApplicationDomainFactory` is constructed and later `newInstance` is called, the selector is available for the SOQL query. |
| **Service wiring** | `IAccountsService.class` → `AccountsServiceImpl` registered in the service factory; `AccountsServiceImpl.rate()` uses `Application.UnitOfWork` and `Application.Selector` as shown in the provided code. |
| **Domain construction** | `Accounts.Constructor` (implementing `fflib_SObjectDomain.IConstructable`) is passed to `registerDomain`, allowing the framework to build an `Accounts` domain from a list of queried records. |
| **Mockability** | All four fields are `public static` with `private set`, and each factory extends the corresponding fflib base class that provides `setMock()`, so tests can call `Application.UnitOfWork.setMock(...)`, etc. |

