```apex
public class AccountOnboardingService {

    /**
     * Creates Accounts (one per map key) and their associated Contacts (one per last name),
     * all through a single fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork.
     *
     * @param contactLastNamesByAccountName  Map of Account Name → list of Contact Last Names.
     * @return The newly created Account records with Ids populated.
     */
    public static List<Account> createAccountsWithContacts(Map<String, List<String>> contactLastNamesByAccountName) {
        if (contactLastNamesByAccountName == null || contactLastNamesByAccountName.isEmpty()) {
            return new List<Account>();
        }

        fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork uow = new fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork();

        List<Account> newAccounts = new List<Account>();
        List<List<Contact>> contactsPerAccount = new List<List<Contact>>();

        // Build all Accounts and their associated Contacts (AccountId not yet set)
        for (String accountName : contactLastNamesByAccountName.keySet()) {
            Account acc = new Account(Name = accountName);
            newAccounts.add(acc);

            List<Contact> accountContacts = new List<Contact>();
            List<String> lastNames = contactLastNamesByAccountName.get(accountName);
            if (lastNames != null) {
                for (String lastName : lastNames) {
                    accountContacts.add(new Contact(LastName = lastName));
                }
            }
            contactsPerAccount.add(accountContacts);
        }

        // Stage all accounts and commit to obtain their Ids
        if (!newAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            uow.createSObject(newAccounts);
            uow.commitWork();
        }

        // Link contacts to their parent accounts and stage them
        List<Contact> allContacts = new List<Contact>();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < newAccounts.size(); i++) {
            for (Contact c : contactsPerAccount[i]) {
                c.AccountId = newAccounts[i].Id;
                allContacts.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (!allContacts.isEmpty()) {
            uow.createSObject(allContacts);
            uow.commitWork();
        }

        return newAccounts;
    }
}
```

**Design notes:**

- **Single UoW instance** – One `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` is created and reused for both the account and contact insertions; no direct DML is issued.
- **Parent–child linking** – Accounts are committed first so their Ids are populated on the same object references; contacts then receive `AccountId` before being staged in a second `commitWork()` call on the *same* UoW. This is the standard fflib pattern for creating parent-child records.
- **Bulk safety** – All accounts are passed as a single `List` to `createSObject(List<…>)`, and all contacts are likewise batched. `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` chunks DML internally to stay within governor limits, so hundreds (or even thousands) of contacts are handled in a small number of bulk-safe `INSERT` calls.
- **Null / empty guard** – A `null` or empty map short-circuits to an empty list with no UoW interaction.

## Analysis

The key requirement is that `Deal_Won__e` events must only be published **after** the DML on opportunities succeeds. The fflib unit of work's `commit()` method handles this perfectly: it calls `save()` first (which throws if DML fails), and only then calls `EventBus.get().publish()`. By registering events via `EventBus.get().register()` (not `publish()`), they sit in the queue until the unit of work commits successfully.

The existing `Application.cls` already registers `Opportunity.SObjectType` in the `UnitOfWork` factory, so no change is needed there.

