public class FollowUpTaskService {

    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate while preserving the input set semantics
        Set<Id> uniqueIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);

        // Single SOQL – respects caller's sharing
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :uniqueIds]
        );

        // IDs that didn't resolve to a queryable Opportunity
        for (Id id : uniqueIds) {
            if (!oppMap.containsKey(id)) {
                failedIds.add(id);
            }
        }

        // Build the task list (and a parallel Id list for result correlation)
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        List<Id>   taskOppIds = new List<Id>();

        for (Id oppId : uniqueIds) {
            Opportunity opp = oppMap.get(oppId);
            if (opp == null) {
                continue; // already recorded as failed above
            }
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = oppId,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status       = 'Not Started',
                Priority     = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskOppIds.add(oppId);
        }

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        // Single bulk DML – allOrNone=false so one rejection doesn't block the rest
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

        // Correlate results back to opportunity IDs (order is preserved)
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedIds.add(taskOppIds[i]);
            }
        }

        return failedIds;
    }
}
```

**Summary of what's different and why:**

1. **One bulk DML instead of N individual inserts.** This is the main change. It keeps us well under the 150-statement DML governor limit even at 250 opportunities, and it's the standard bulk-safe pattern Salesforce expects.

2. **`Database.insert(list, false)` instead of try/catch around `insert`.** With `allOrNone = false`, the call never throws for individual record rejections; you simply check each `SaveResult`. The isolation guarantee is identical — a compliance validation rule rejecting one task does not prevent the other 249 from being created.

3. **Parallel `taskOppIds` list for result correlation.** `SaveResult` doesn't carry a reference back to the source record, so we keep a positional index. (You could also read `WhatId` off the Task, but the parallel list is more explicit and avoids an extra dereference.)

4. **Unresolvable IDs are reported as failures.** If an ID in the input doesn't match a visible Opportunity (deleted, or hidden by sharing), it goes into the returned set so the caller knows it wasn't actioned.

