public class AccountRatingService {

    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        // Current ratings so we can skip accounts that haven't changed
        Map<Id, String> currentRatings = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Account acc : [
            SELECT Id, Rating
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ]) {
            currentRatings.put(acc.Id, acc.Rating);
        }

        // Open opportunities grouped by account
        Map<Id, List<Opportunity>> oppsByAccount = new Map<Id, List<Opportunity>>();
        for (Opportunity opp : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, Amount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds AND IsClosed = false
        ]) {
            if (!oppsByAccount.containsKey(opp.AccountId)) {
                oppsByAccount.put(opp.AccountId, new List<Opportunity>());
            }
            oppsByAccount.get(opp.AccountId).add(opp);
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            String newRating;
            List<Opportunity> openOpps = oppsByAccount.get(accountId);

            if (openOpps == null || openOpps.isEmpty()) {
                newRating = 'Cold';
            } else {
                Boolean hasHot = false;
                for (Opportunity opp : openOpps) {
                    if (opp.Amount != null && opp.Amount >= 100000) {
                        hasHot = true;
                        break;
                    }
                }
                newRating = hasHot ? 'Hot' : 'Warm';
            }

            // Only DML when the rating actually changes
            if (newRating != currentRatings.get(accountId)) {
                accountsToUpdate.add(new Account(Id = accountId, Rating = newRating));
            }
        }

        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

## What I'd change and why

The instruction says to call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` in a loop, but that method executes a **SOQL query on every call**. With 200 lines per quote, that's 200 queries in one transaction — exceeding the **100 SOQL queries** governor limit and throwing a `LimitException`.

**My fix:** Pre-query all needed `Product2` records in a **single** SOQL, build an `Id → Family` map, then call `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` for each line. This:

- Keeps the discount rules entirely inside `PricingRules` (I'm not copying the if/else logic).
- Uses exactly **1** SOQL regardless of line count.
- Still delegates the "which discount for which family" decision to `PricingRules`.

The only difference from the naïve loop is that I resolve the product's `Family` myself (one batched query) instead of letting `standardDiscountFor` do it one-at-a-time.

