public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * Before-insert: apply the standard product discount to any line
     * whose Discount field is still null. Lines that already carry a
     * discount (including 0) are left untouched.
     */
    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        // 1. Identify lines that need a default discount
        List<OpportunityLineItem> linesNeedingDiscount = new List<OpportunityLineItem>();
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                linesNeedingDiscount.add(line);
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (linesNeedingDiscount.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. Single SOQL to resolve Family for every distinct product
        Map<Id, String> productFamilyMap = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Product2 p : [
            SELECT Id, Family
            FROM Product2
            WHERE Id IN :productIds
        ]) {
            productFamilyMap.put(p.Id, p.Family);
        }

        // 3. Delegate the discount rule to PricingRules (one call per line, no SOQL inside)
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : linesNeedingDiscount) {
            String family = productFamilyMap.get(line.Product2Id);
            line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family);
        }
    }
}
```

## Approach

I'd do it differently: instead of a nested O(n × m) loop, I'll build a `Map<String, Id>` keyed by the normalized email in one pass over `contacts` (first occurrence wins), then do a single O(1) lookup per lead. The total work drops from ~9 million string comparisons (each involving `trim().toLowerCase()` allocations) to ~6,000 operations.

**Why this is still readable:** the two loops are independent and each has a single clear purpose ("index contacts by email" / "resolve each lead"). You don't lose anything in clarity, and you eliminate the quadratic behaviour that would bite if the region ever grows or if someone reuses this for a larger batch.

