## Feedback on your approach

**The critical problem: the 10-email governor limit.**

`Messaging.sendEmail` with `SingleEmailMessage` (non-gated email) is capped at **10 emails per Apex transaction**, regardless of how many separate `sendEmail` calls you make. With up to 200 opportunities in one call, you will hit `LimitExceeded: EMAIL: 10` partway through the loop. The remaining emails silently never go out (or the whole transaction rolls back if the exception is unhandled).

**Recommended fix:** Have the scheduled job enqueue a `Queueable` (or use a `Database.Batchable`) that processes opportunities in chunks of 10. Each `Queueable` invocation is its own transaction, so each gets its own 10-email allocation. Example sketch:

```apex
public class RenewalReminderBatch implements Queueable {
    private List<Id> ids;
    private Integer start;

    public RenewalReminderBatch(List<Id> ids, Integer start) {
        this.ids = ids;
        this.start = start;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        Integer end = Math.min(start + 10, ids.size());
        List<Id> chunk = ids.sublist(start, end);
        RenewalReminderService.sendReminders(chunk);

        if (end < ids.size()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new RenewalReminderBatch(ids, end));
        }
    }
}
```

The scheduled job would then do `System.enqueueJob(new RenewalReminderBatch(allIds, 0))` instead of calling `sendReminders` directly.

**Minor point on "one call per opportunity":** Your instinct that a single failure shouldn't block the rest is correct, but `Messaging.sendEmail` with an array already returns per-email success/failure results without aborting the others. So within a 10-email chunk you could pass all 10 in one `sendEmail` call and still inspect each `SendEmailResult` independently. The one-call-per-email pattern is not wrong, it just uses a slightly higher per-call overhead for no additional resilience benefit.

## Why I'm changing your approach

Your proposed query — `SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact` with no `WHERE` clause — will silently fail in production. Salesforce caps any single SOQL result set at **50,000 rows** (both synchronous and asynchronous contexts). With 2.3 million contacts you'd get an arbitrary, non-deterministic slice of 50k rows and miss the remaining ~2.25 million. The trigger would appear to "work" in a small sandbox but silently skip contacts in prod.

The fix is to **filter the query to only the accounts that actually changed** in the current transaction:

```
SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
```

This is still **one SOQL statement**, still no queries in loops, and it scales correctly: with MDM batches of 200 accounts you'll typically pull back a few hundred to a few thousand contacts — well within limits — regardless of total org contact volume.

Everything else about your design (one query, map-based lookup, after-update trigger) is sound and is preserved below.

