public class HouseholdService {

    public static Id createHousehold(List<Contact> members) {
        if (members == null || members.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException('members must not be null or empty');
        }

        // Look up the Household Account record type by developer name (no hard-coded Id)
        RecordType rt = [
            SELECT Id
            FROM RecordType
            WHERE SObjectType = 'Account'
              AND DeveloperName = 'HH_Account'
            LIMIT 1
        ];

        // Create the household account — leave Name unset so NPSP's naming
        // formula/trigger populates it automatically once contacts are linked.
        Account hh = new Account(RecordTypeId = rt.Id);
        insert hh;

        // Point every contact at the new household before inserting so NPSP
        // never spins up a separate (empty) household for any of them.
        for (Contact c : members) {
            c.AccountId = hh.Id;
        }
        insert members;

        // Designate the first contact as the household's primary contact.
        hh.npe01__One2OneContact__c = members[0].Id;
        update hh;

        return hh.Id;
    }
}
```

**Why this works:**

| Concern | How it's addressed |
|---|---|
| **Single household, no strays** | We create the Account *first*, then stamp its Id onto every Contact *before* the `insert`. NPSP's triggers see each contact already linked to an existing household, so no extra households are created. |
| **Automatic naming** | We never assign `Account.Name`. NPSP's household-naming trigger/formula fires when the contacts are inserted and sets the name (e.g. "Smith, John and Maria"). |
| **Primary contact** | After the contacts have Ids, we write `npe01__One2OneContact__c = members[0].Id` in a single update. |
| **No hard-coded RT Id** | The record type is resolved at runtime via a SOQL on `RecordType` using the DeveloperName. |
| **Guard** | `IllegalArgumentException` is thrown for null or empty input before any DML or SOQL runs. |

The method performs exactly three DML statements (insert account → insert contacts → update account), which is safe within Salesforce's governor limits for the single-family-per-call use case described.

